### How is this different from Longest Palindromic Substring?

| Aspect | Palindromic Subsequence | Palindromic Substring |
|---|---|---|
| Contiguity | Characters may be non-contiguous | Characters must be contiguous |
| Approach | Interval DP or LCS with reverse | Center expansion or Manacher's algorithm |
| Time | O(n^2) | O(n^2) center expansion, O(n) Manacher's |
| Difficulty | Harder because skipping is allowed | Easier with center expansion |

The substring version can use center expansion because contiguous characters form a window that expands outward. The subsequence version cannot, because non-contiguous characters require the full interval DP.

```java
// Longest Palindromic SUBSTRING via center expansion (contrast)
public static String longestPalindromeSubstring(String s) {
    int start = 0, maxLen = 1;
    for (int center = 0; center < s.length(); center++) {
        // Odd length
        int lo = center, hi = center;
        while (lo >= 0 && hi < s.length() && s.charAt(lo) == s.charAt(hi)) {
            if (hi - lo + 1 > maxLen) { start = lo; maxLen = hi - lo + 1; }
            lo--; hi++;
        }
        // Even length
        lo = center; hi = center + 1;
        while (lo >= 0 && hi < s.length() && s.charAt(lo) == s.charAt(hi)) {
            if (hi - lo + 1 > maxLen) { start = lo; maxLen = hi - lo + 1; }
            lo--; hi++;
        }
    }
    return s.substring(start, start + maxLen);
}
```

### Why is this equivalent to LCS of the string with its reverse?

The longest palindromic subsequence of a string S is exactly the longest common subsequence (LCS) of S and its reverse. A subsequence that reads the same forwards and backwards must appear in both S and reverse(S), and the longest such common subsequence is by definition the longest palindromic subsequence.

This gives an alternative implementation using the standard LCS algorithm.

```java
public static int lpsViaLCS(String code) {
    String reversed = new StringBuilder(code).reverse().toString();
    int n = code.length();
    int[][] dp = new int[n + 1][n + 1];
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
            if (code.charAt(i - 1) == reversed.charAt(j - 1)) {
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
            } else {
                dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
            }
        }
    }
    return dp[n][n];
}
```

### What is the fill order and why does it matter?

The cell `dp[i][j]` depends on `dp[i+1][j-1]`, `dp[i+1][j]`, and `dp[i][j-1]`. All three are from smaller intervals. To ensure they are ready, either iterate i from the end backward and j forward, or iterate by increasing interval length.

Iterating by length is the clearest approach: length 1 (base cases), then length 2, then length 3, up to length n.

```java
// Fill order: increasing interval length
for (int len = 2; len <= n; len++) {       // length of window
    for (int i = 0; i <= n - len; i++) {   // left end
        int j = i + len - 1;               // right end
        // dp[i][j] uses dp[i+1][j-1], dp[i+1][j], dp[i][j-1]
        // all have length < len, so already computed
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

- **Empty string** – return 0.
- **Single character** – return 1, it is its own palindrome.
- **All same characters** – the entire string is a palindrome, return n.
- **No repeated characters** – every character stands alone, return 1.
- **Already a palindrome** – the LPS length equals the string length.

```java
// Edge case: all same characters
String allSame = "AAAA";
// dp fills: every pair matches, dp[0][3] = 4
System.out.println(longestPalindromicSubsequence(allSame)); // 4
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Time is O(n^2) because the table has n^2 cells and each takes O(1) to fill. Space is O(n^2) for the table. Space can be reduced to O(n) by observing that each row depends only on the next row, but the implementation is trickier.

For banking reference codes of typical length (10-20 characters), this is instantaneous. For very long strings (thousands of characters), the quadratic space might matter, and the rolling-row optimisation becomes relevant.

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is confusing subsequence with substring and using center expansion, which only finds contiguous palindromes.

```java
// WRONG: using center expansion for subsequence
// Center expansion finds "BCB" in "ABCBA" but misses "ABCBA" if chars are non-contiguous
// It cannot handle skipping characters

// CORRECT: use interval DP for subsequence
// dp[i][j] properly considers skipping characters by dropping one end
```

Another mistake is the fill order. If you iterate i forward and j forward, `dp[i+1][j-1]` might not be computed yet.

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know the LCS equivalence, because it connects two seemingly different problems and shows that palindromic subsequence is just a special case of the general LCS framework. They should also know that recovering the actual subsequence (not just its length) requires backtracking through the DP table, following the choices that led to each cell's value.
